Lucero is the tenth planet from the star Aurelius, orbiting far beyond the tilted giant [[Alcázar]]. It is the first of the two ice giants in the outer system, distinct from the gas giants for having a higher proportion of ices such as water, ammonia, and methane in its composition. It is slightly more massive than its neighbor [[Centinela]], making it the fourth-largest planet in the system by mass.

Lucero is famous for its brilliant, featureless appearance. Its atmosphere is remarkably serene and is shrouded in a thick, uniform layer of high-altitude methane ice clouds. These clouds scatter virtually all incident sunlight, giving the planet an incredibly high albedo and making it appear as a glowing, milky-white pearl against the blackness of space.

Because of its high reflectivity and immense distance, Lucero appears as a steady, bright point of light in the Adonian sky, earning it the historical nickname the Morning Star or Evening Star depending on its position relative to the sun. It is the coldest of the major planets, with an effective temperature that hovers just above absolute zero.

==Discovery==
Historically, Lucero was categorized as a fixed star of the sixth magnitude by ancient Sinopian and Sanese astronomers. Because of its exceptionally slow orbital motion, its planetary nature remained unrecognized for centuries, often being confused with distant luminaries in the constellation of the Weaver. The formal discovery was made on the night of March 13, 1781 (Adonian Era), by the Insulonian astronomer Donato de Silva (ドナート・デ・シルバ).

Working from the Royal Observatory of Nueva Esperanza, de Silva was engaged in a systematic survey of the celestial equator when he identified a luminous point that exhibited a distinct, non-stellar disc. Subsequent observations over several weeks confirmed that the object followed a nearly circular orbit beyond [[Alcázar]]. The discovery significantly expanded the known boundaries of the Aurelius system and established de Silva as a pioneer of telescopic astronomy on [[Adonia]].

==Physical characteristics==
===Atmosphere===
The atmosphere of Lucero is colder and calmer than any other giant planet in the system. The visible cloud deck is composed almost entirely of methane ice crystals, which form a thick, uniform haze that hides any deeper atmospheric structures. Below this haze, models suggest layers of ammonia ice and water ice clouds.

Due to the extreme cold and lack of significant internal heat sources, Lucero's atmosphere exhibits very little convective activity. There are no visible storms, bands, or spots. The weather patterns that do exist are driven by slow, massive zonal winds that redistribute the meager solar energy received at this distance.

===Internal structure===
Unlike the gas giants which are mostly hydrogen and helium, Lucero is composed primarily of heavier elements. It is believed to have a small rocky core surrounded by a massive mantle of superionic water, ammonia, and methane ices that make up two-thirds of its total mass. Above this icy mantle is a relatively thin atmosphere of hydrogen and helium enriched with methane.

==Orbit and rotation==
Lucero orbits Aurelius at an average distance of 22.80 AU, completing a single revolution every 42,500 standard days (approximately 116 Adonian years). Its orbit is nearly circular and has very little inclination relative to the system's invariable plane.

The planet rotates on its axis once every 18.2 hours. Its axial tilt is 22 degrees, similar to [[Adonia]], meaning it experiences distinct, albeit incredibly long and cold, seasons. Each season on Lucero lasts for approximately 29 Adonian years.

==Name and etymology==
The name Lucero comes from the Insulonian word for a bright star, specifically the morning star, reflecting its brilliance in the night sky. Ancient astronomers, noting its unblinking light, revered it as a symbol of guidance and purity.

In Sanese astronomical nomenclature, the planet is known as Byakuyasei (白夜星 Byakuyasei), translating to the White Night Star. This name is a poetic reference to its ghostly, glowing white appearance, which seemed to turn the darkest nights into a twilight.

